        "thinking": "I see - `express-router-bridge.ts` is Workers-safe (only imports types from the server package). But the issue is that `api.ts` imports the route factory functions like `companyRoutes`, which are Express-based. When those functions are imported and called, they create Express Router instances, which requires Express to be available.\n\nThe idea was that Express would be bundled in but its runtime behavior (connecting to Node HTTP server) would never happen. The issue is that even importing Express causes `node:http` to be required.\n\nNow, let me look at the approach from a different angle. The error says:\n```\nNo such module \"node:http\"\n\n\n```\n\nIn the CF Workers runtime with `nodejs_compat`, `node:http` is NOT available. But with Wrangler 3, the `nodejs_compat` flag is supposed to provide some Node.js built-ins. Looking at Cloudflare's documentation, the available Node.js APIs include `assert`, `buffer`, `crypto`, `events`, `module`, `path`, `process`, `stream`, `string_decoder`, `url`, `util`, and `timers` — but notably NOT `http`, `https`, `net`, `child_process`, `fs`, or `os`.\n\nSo Express won't work because it depends on `node:http`. But the existing setup was designed to extract just the route definitions without needing the HTTP layer itself — the route factory functions create Express routers, but what I actually need are the `__handler` functions they define. The question is whether importing `Router` from Express even triggers the `node:http` dependency, or if that only happens when you try to actually use the HTTP server.\n\nLooking at how Express loads, importing `Router` pulls in the entire package including `request.js` and `response.js`, both of which depend on `node:http`. So there's no way around it — just importing Express loads the Node.js HTTP module.\n\nI'm considering a few approaches: mocking `node:http` to provide stub implementations of `IncomingMessage` and `ServerResponse`, marking Express as external so it doesn't bundle but then losing the ability to create routers at runtime, switching to Hono entirely for the CF build path, or creating lightweight shims that satisfy Express's requirements without actual Node.js HTTP.\n\nThe mock approach seems most viable since Express only uses these classes as prototype bases for its own request and response objects — the actual HTTP server functionality never gets called. But I'm realizing this is getting pretty hacky, and I should step back and think about whether using Express routers in a CF Workers environment is even the right approach here.\n\nThe core issue is that the route factory functions need to instantiate Express routers, which immediately fails because Express depends on Node.js HTTP APIs. What if instead of trying to stub out the entire HTTP module, I provide a minimal shim that just gives Express enough to bootstrap its Router class? I could inject this shim through esbuild or configure it in wrangler.toml to make `node:http` available without actually needing Node.js. globals.\n\nActually, let me look at what Wrangler's `nodejs_compat` flag does internally. When you use `nodejs_compat`, Wrangler (and the CF runtime) provide certain Node.js built-ins. Some, like `node:crypto`, are full implementations. Others are polyfills.\n\nThe key is: `node:http` is NOT provided by `nodejs_compat` because it represents the HTTP server API (binding to a port etc.) which makes no sense in a serverless environment.\n\nHOWEVER - Express v5 uses `node:http` only for the IncomingMessage and ServerResponse base classes. These are used as prototype bases for request and response objects. But in the CF worker, we're not creating HTTP requests via Express - we're creating Express Router stacks and walking them to extract route handlers.\n\nWhen we call `companyRoutes(db, storage)`, it creates a Router, registers routes on it with middleware functions, and returns the router. The Router creation doesn't spin up an HTTP server—it just builds a routing object. The problem is that importing Express loads `node:http` before we even call Router.\n\nWhat if we provide a minimal `node:http` shim in the bundle with just empty classes? Instead of marking `node:http` as external, we could use esbuild's `alias` or `inject` option to swap in a stub that exports the IncomingMessage and ServerResponse classes Express needs...\n\nWriting HTTP stub... I'm realizing the route files still export Express Router instances wrapped with `expressHandler`, so they're not truly transport-agnostic yet. The real solution would be to have each route file export plain route definition objects instead, which would eliminate the need to instantiate Express at build time. I'm reconsidering the approach—maybe I can create minimal shims for the Node.js APIs that Express depends on rather than refactoring all the route files. Looking at Cloudflare's `nodejs_compat` flag, it actually provides quite a few built-in Node.js modules like `events`, `buffer`, `crypto`, and `util`, so I might not need to stub everything from scratch. I'm realizing this approach is getting too complex, so let me reconsider the whole strategy. The cleanest path forward is probably just stubbing out the few Node.js APIs that Express actually needs at initialization time—`IncomingMessage`, `ServerResponse`, `METHODS`, and `STATUS_CODES`—since they're never actually instantiated at runtime in a Cloudflare Worker. I can use esbuild aliases to redirect those imports to shim files, or even simpler, create a minimal mock Express implementation that only exposes the routing layer without the HTTP server dependencies.\n\nActually, let me just focus on the absolute minimum needed to get `wrangler dev` working: stub files for `node:http`, `node:net`, and `node:fs` that provide just enough for Express to load and create routers without crashing. Looking at how the routes are actually constructed, `buildRouteCache` only runs on the first request, not at startup. But the worker is failing to start, which means the problem happens during module import itself—before any request even arrives. So something in the module initialization is triggering the error, not the route building logic.\n\nThe culprit is that the route files use static imports at the top level, and when those files load, they immediately import Express's Router, which in turn requires `node:http`. Since `node:http` isn't available in Cloudflare Workers, the import fails right away.\n\nI'm considering a few approaches to fix this: the simplest would be to create a stub for `node:http` that provides minimal empty classes so Express can initialize without crashing, or I could explore other options like lazy-loading the routes or using a different approach entirely.\n\nActually, dynamic imports in `api.ts` wouldn't help here since `node:http` still wouldn't be available even at request time in CF Workers. Let me think about whether there's something I'm missing about how the compatibility layer works.\n\nLooking at the `wrangler.toml`, I can see `nodejs_compat` is already enabled, but that doesn't include `node:http` support—it's a hard platform limitation. So I need to go with creating a stub module for `node:http` that provides empty implementations instead.\n\nExpress only needs a few things from `node:http`: the `IncomingMessage` and `ServerResponse` classes (which it uses for prototyping), plus `METHODS` and `STATUS_CODES`. Since the route extraction code just walks the `.stack` properties and never actually instantiates these objects, minimal stubs should be sufficient to get the router initialized.\n\nBut there's a bigger problem—Express and the service code import many other Node modules like `node:net`, `node:crypto`, `node:fs`, `node:child_process`, and `node:stream`. These will all fail the same way unless stubbed, and since they're marked as external in the build, they'll appear as bare imports in the bundle that Miniflare needs to handle.\n\nMiniflare's nodejs_compat provides some of these (crypto, events, stream, path) but not others (http, net, fs, child_process, os, tls). So I need to stub the missing ones.\n\nRather than marking them as external, I should use esbuild's alias feature to replace these Node modules with stub implementations at build time.\n\nLooking at how wrangler handles this, it uses the `unenv` package which provides pre-built stubs for Node.js APIs. That's probably the cleanest approach here too.\n\nActually, I think the real solution is simpler — instead of trying to polyfill everything, I should just stub out Express itself. The route files only need `Router()` to create and register routes, so I can create a minimal Express stub that provides just that function without pulling in any Node.js HTTP dependencies. This way the route factories can run in the worker without trying to import the full Express library. I see that the router files use Express's standard API—creating a router instance, registering routes with methods like `get()` and `post()`, and passing handlers. So my shim needs to support that same interface and build the internal stack structure that the bridge expects. This approach avoids any Node.js dependencies while maintaining compatibility with the existing route definitions.",
